  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nRain showers are in full swing this afternoon, and will \ncontinue tonight through Tuesday and Wednesday. The heaviest \nrainfall is still expected this afternoon and evening with this \ninitial wave of moisture pushing through our region ahead of a \ndeepening low pressure system. A few embedded rumbles of thunder\nremain possible across parts of the Northland through this \nevening. Strong southeasterly winds will gust between 25 and 35 \nmph before easing later tonight. As a dry conveyor belt works \ninto the area overnight, widespread rain will transition to more\nscattered showers. This will lead to a lull in rain intensity \ntonight before a second round follows with another shortwave on\nTuesday. Given ongoing D1 to D4 drought conditions across the \nNorthland, this soaking rain brings welcome relief.\n\nWidespread soaking rain will redevelop across the area on Tuesday\nas a shortwave dives south from northern Manitoba and reinforces\nthe trough over the Upper Great Lakes, drawing an influx of Gulf\nmoisture into the region. Rainfall will expand in coverage by\nTuesday afternoon and turn heavy at times heading into Tuesday\nnight. Orographic enhancement over the North Shore will likely lead\nto an additional 1.5-2 inches after this evening, while most others\nwill only see an inch to inch and a half. Rain showers will\ncontinue through Tuesday night across the entire Northland, with\nelevated instability supporting an isolated thunderstorm risk in\nparts of northwest Wisconsin.\n\nThe low pressure system will briefly slow over the area\nthroughout Wednesday, causing showers to continue through\nWednesday. Rain will remain persistent and widespread across our\nregion before gradually tapering from west to east late Wednesday\nnight as the center of the system begins a sluggish shift\ndownstream. Multi-day storm total rainfall will broadly reach 1 to\n2 inches across the Northland, with localized totals exceeding 2 to\n3 inches in north-central Minnesota and along the higher terrain of\nthe North Shore. Winds will veer northwesterly Wednesday afternoon\nas a cooler airmass begins filtering into western areas.\n\nOn the back end of the second system, northwest flow of cooler\nair over Lake Superior will lead to a few light showers over\nAshland and Iron counties into Thursday. For the rest of the\nNorthland, rain will taper off early Thursday morning as the low\ndeparts. A noticeably cooler airmass will overspread the region on\ngusty northwest winds, causing daytime temperatures to struggle in\nthe lower to mid 40s. High pressure begins building into the Upper\nMidwest late Thursday and maintains control through Friday. This\nbrings an end to the active wet pattern and allows some sunshine to\nbreak through mostly cloudy skies.\n\nDrier and quiet conditions will persist into the weekend as high\npressure shifts eastward across the Great Lakes. Saturday will\nremain largely precipitation-free with partly sunny to mostly\ncloudy skies. A weak upper-level disturbance will introduce a\nslight 20 percent chance of light rain showers Saturday night into\nSunday, though no significant accumulation is anticipated. Quiet\nweather returns Sunday afternoon with seasonably cool air\nmaintaining overnight lows down into the upper 20s and lower\n30s.",
